\fBlock\fR guards \fBjobs\fR, \fBrunning\fR, \fBactiveThreads\fR, \fBaliveThreads\fR and the first error. Workers sleep on \fBhasWork\fR; wait() sleeps on \fBnoWork\fR\&.
.SH CONSTANTS
.SS ERR_THREAD
.EX
0x6B746D40
.EE
.PP
The code of the Error thread.spawn throws when no thread can be started
.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS channel
.EX
Channel<T> channel<T>(i64 cap = 0);
.EE
.PP
A Channel holding up to cap values (0: unbuffered)
.SS go
.EX
void go<T>(fn(T *) void f, T *arg, WaitGroup *wg);
void go(fn() void f, WaitGroup *wg);
.EE
.PP
Runs f(arg) on a new thread that wg waits for: go() adds 1 to wg and the thread calls wg.done() when f returns. There is nothing to join: what f throws, wg.wait() throws. Keep arg alive until wg.wait() returns. Throws ERR_THREAD when no thread can be started.
.PP
Runs f() on a new thread that wg waits for
.SS each
.EX
void each<T>(T[] items, fn(T *) void f);
.EE
.PP
Calls f(&items[i]) for every item, the items split over as many threads as the machine has cores (one per item for fewer items), and returns when all are done. Throws the first error f threw. f gets each item once, from one thread; items that f changes are shared with nothing else, but anything else f touches needs a lock.
.SS spawn
.EX
Thread spawn<T>(fn(T *) void f, T *arg);
Thread spawn(fn() void f);
.EE
.PP
Runs f(arg) on a new thread. arg is shared with the caller: keep it alive, and don't change it from both sides without a Mutex, until join() returns. Throws ERR_THREAD when no thread can be started.
.PP
Runs f() on a new thread
.SS poolEnqueue
.EX
void poolEnqueue(Pool *pool, void *argv, fn(void *privData, void *argv) void callback);
.EE
.PP
Queues callback(pool.privData, argv). Old name, kept for one minor: use Pool.run
.SS poolWait
.EX
void poolWait(Pool *pool);
.EE
.PP
Waits until every queued job is done. Old name, kept for one minor: use Pool.wait
.SS poolNew
.EX
Pool *poolNew(i64 workerCount);
.EE
.PP
A pool of workerCount threads; null if workerCount <= 0 or a thread can't be created (the ones already started are stopped). Old name, kept for one minor: use thread.pool
.SS poolStop
.EX
void poolStop(Pool *pool);
.EE
.PP
Finishes every queued job, then stops and joins the workers. Old name, kept for one minor: use Pool.free
.SS poolRelease
.EX
void poolRelease(Pool *pool);
.EE
.PP
Stops the pool (queued jobs still run, see thread.poolStop) and frees it. Old name, kept for one minor: use Pool.free
.SS pool
.EX
Pool *pool(i64 n = 0);
.EE
.PP
A Pool of n threads (n <= 0: one per core). free() stops it: the functions already given still run. Throws ERR_THREAD when a thread can't be started.
